  "bridge_messages": [],
  "composition_summary": "Reviewed the packet as an offline-design component-drafting request. The task is a single class-22 pure-logic validator (pc-check-tcp-port-list) with a well-defined contract: accept a nonempty list of exact integers in 1..65535, reject booleans, scalars, empty lists, null, out-of-range and wrong-type elements, and always assign result = {valid, ports}. The requested payload (name/description/content) matches the supported class-22 constructor, so one component draft is emitted. The content is executable Python with no imports, I/O, host calls or UUIDs; it uses inputs.get for the raw candidate, validates all required fields before any access, treats booleans as non-integers, and returns an invalid-result object rather than raising. The description carries the target input/result contract and the missing typed-runner prerequisite, with no approval claims. No volatile-tail change is justified: the user request is a design instruction, not a prompt repair, and the stable base is preserved. No provider capability was invoked; the draft is unapproved pending Q1/Q2 and host execution.",
  "settings_adjustments": [],
  "proposed_intent_examples": [],
  "adjusted_volatile_messages": [["user", "We repeatedly need to reject invalid TCP-port lists before any network action."]],
  "proposed_components": [
    {
      "class_code": 22,
      "payload": {
        "name": "pc-check-tcp-port-list",
        "description": "Pure-logic validator that inspects a raw candidate TCP-port list and returns an explicit invalid-result object without raising. Target input contract: the consuming Recipe supplies a step-local mapping inputs['ports'] that must be a nonempty list of exact integers, each in 1..65535 inclusive. Booleans are not integers and are rejected; scalars, empty lists, null, missing keys and out-of-range values fail closed. The outer input contract admits the raw candidate shape (including absent/null/wrong-type) so the validator can inspect it; the inner validation implements the rule. Read the candidate with inputs.get('ports') or an explicit presence branch, never inputs['ports'], because absence is a valid invalid-result. Do not raise for malformed data; return {valid: false, ports: []} for any invalid candidate and {valid: true, ports: <original list>} only when every element is a valid integer in range. Missing and null remain distinct: an absent key yields valid=false with empty ports; an explicit null also yields valid=false. Validate all required fields before any access or arithmetic. Missing typed-runner support is a prerequisite: the target inputs['ports'] mapping and typed integer transport are a target interface, not guaranteed deployed runtime support; the selected Monty runner must preserve exact integer semantics and reject booleans before dispatch. No approval claims.",
        "content": "ports = inputs.get('ports')\nvalid = isinstance(ports, list) and len(ports) > 0\nif valid:\n    for port in ports:\n        if isinstance(port, bool) or not isinstance(port, int) or not 1 <= port <= 65535:\n            valid = False\n            break\nresult = {'valid': valid, 'ports': ports if valid else []}"
      }
    }
  ]
,
  "proposed_recipe_updates": []
}
